  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1202 AM CDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nCold front is almost completely across the CWA with dewpoints in the \n50s upstream replacing 70s. Convection  in the post frontal airmass \ncontinues east and weakens through the next few hours.\n\nWeak surface high pressure overhead today, with westerly low level \nflow keeping us dry, but no longer cold advecting aloft. An upper \nlow travels across the southern tier of Canada, with a tighter \ngradient up in North Dakota, down here mixed winds only top out in \nthe low 20s (kts) for KMBG and lower elsewhere. Once the upper low \nmoves east, an elongated ridge develops extending out of the Bermuda \nhigh centered over the southeast. Embedded within this ridge is a \nband of moisture aloft, coming up out of the tropical Pacific/sea \nof Cortez region and this is our next round of moisture. \n\nNAM BUFKIT profiles show mainly most adiabatic profiles, with NAM \nPWATS around 1.5 to 1.75 inches and the GEFS closer to a mean of \n1.25 to 1.5 but still right at the limit of 2 standard deviations \nabove climo. Southwesterly flow at 500mb is still fairly strong (30-\n50kts), however it appears a low level flow is east southeasterly \nwith a weak low, associated with a subtle southwest flow shortwave \ncoming in behind the mid level plume of moisture.    \n\nOnce that system progresses out of the region, a trough over the \nwestern CONUS becomes the main defining feature of the upper level \nflow. Its nearly stationary, with southwest flow aloft overhead \nunder a broad ridge that develops in response.  That ridge gradually \ntransitions into the upper level high, all of this shutting us down \nfor moisture. All that time, mid level temperatures are on the \nincrease, topping out around +13 to +15C at 700mb, about a \nstandard deviation above climo. \n\n850mb temperatures are likewise about a standard deviation above \nclimo, with NBM highs/lows running about 5 to 10 degrees above \nnormal, with the warmest days Friday/Saturday with readings 10 to 20 \ndegrees above.",
